---
/**
 * The `figure` block, as a Portable Text type serializer. Three shapes from one
 * component, selected by two booleans an editor sets in the Studio:
 *
 *   default            full prose width, capped at the measure by base.css
 *   outline: true      the same, with a hairline border — for white-background
 *                      images that would otherwise dissolve into the page
 *   thumbnail: true    150px, floated left in an article body with the prose
 *                      wrapping around it. The book-cover treatment.
 *
 * They compose: a thumbnail can take an outline, which is in fact the common case
 * for a book cover photographed against white.
 *
 * ── IT REPLACES A SECOND BLOCK TYPE ──────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `article.bodyText` also allows a bare inline `image` whose `floatLeft` flag did
 * the thumbnail job. That type is being retired in favour of this one (2026-08-26)
 * so there is one serializer rather than two near-identical ones, and so a cover
 * gains the `caption` field the bare image never had. The `image` type is still in
 * the schema while the 11 existing blocks are converted by hand — until then those
 * render nothing and the build says `missing "image"` on every run, which is a
 * useful progress indicator rather than a fault to suppress.
 *
 * ── THE TOP ALIGNMENT IS AN EXPLICIT RULE, NOT A FREE ONE ────────────────────
 *
 * The whole point of the treatment is that the cover's top edge and the book
 * title's first line start together, and the rhythm ramp decides that: a float is
 * placed where it would have sat in flow, so the alignment is an equality between
 * the figure's top margin and the following `h3`'s.
 *
 * THIS BLOCK USED TO SAY THE TWO MATCHED FOR FREE, and for a while they did —
 * `* + :is(ul, ol, blockquote, figure, pre)` gave the figure 48 and `* + h3` gives
 * the heading 48. That pair was removed on 2026-09-02 for good reasons that had
 * nothing to do with covers, the figure fell to the base `* + *` at 24, and every
 * cover on the reading-list article rode 24px above its title until it was caught
 * on screen.
 *
 * base.css now states the coupling instead of relying on it — one rule beside
 * `--thumb-float`, reading the same token as the heading step. Recorded here
 * because THIS comment is what made the dependency look self-maintaining.
 *
 * The residue is optical, not structural: a heading's line box carries about 7px
 * of half-leading plus ascent above its capitals, so the cover's hard top edge
 * sits marginally above the title's caps. That is an eye-set nudge if it wants
 * one — CSS exposes no font metric to derive it, the same wall the masthead's nav
 * baseline hit in phase 3 — and it is deliberately left alone until judged on
 * screen.
 *
 * ── NO `aspect`, DELIBERATELY ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Absent means "keep the source's own shape", which is right for both a diagram
 * and a book cover: the figures in the real articles run 2400x914 and 1920x1440
 * and 596x904, and forcing a shared ratio would crop a diagram's own edges off.
 * It also means the hotspot is inert, which is correct — nothing is being
 * discarded for it to protect. A crop set in the Studio still applies; that
 * happens in the URL builder and needs no aspect.
 */
import type {Props as NodeProps} from 'astro-portabletext/types'
import SanityImage from '../SanityImage.astro'
import type {SanityImageSource} from '../../sanity/image'

type FigureNode = SanityImageSource & {
  _type: 'figure'
  _key?: string
  outline?: boolean | null
  thumbnail?: boolean | null
}

type Props = NodeProps<FigureNode>

const {node} = Astro.props

/**
 * Data attributes rather than classes, matching <Band>'s `data-tone` / `data-space`.
 * Emitted only when true, so the selectors below stay simple attribute presence
 * tests and the markup carries no `data-thumbnail="false"` noise.
 */
const isThumb = node.thumbnail === true
const hasOutline = node.outline === true
---

<figure data-thumbnail={isThumb ? '' : undefined} data-outline={hasOutline ? '' : undefined}>
  <SanityImage image={node} />
  {node.caption && <figcaption>{node.caption}</figcaption>}
</figure>

<style>
  /*
   * ── The thumbnail ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * `float` and `clear` both read `--thumb-float`, which base.css declares on
   * `article.detail` at lg and nowhere else. So this component never asks where it
   * is: an article body hands it `left`, a case study hands it nothing and the
   * `none` fallback applies, and below lg the property is not declared at all.
   *
   * `clear` taking the same value is not a trick for its own sake — it is exactly
   * right. Wherever the figure floats it must also clear the previous float, or
   * two consecutive book covers sit side by side when an entry runs short.
   */
  figure[data-thumbnail] {
    /*
     * 150px at the nominal root. In `rem` for the same reason the hero's height
     * cap is: the measure beside it is set in `ch`, which scales with the reader's
     * text size, so a thumbnail fixed in px would eat a growing share of a
     * scaled-up column. The literal is exact rather than rounded — 9.375 x 16 =
     * 150 — matching how the type scale carries its own odd values.
     */
    inline-size: 9.375rem;

    float: var(--thumb-float, none);
    clear: var(--thumb-float, none);

    /*
     * The gap between cover and text. `grid-gutter-content` is DESIGN.md's token
     * for "a real gap between content columns", and that is what this is — the
     * thumbnail and the prose beside it read as two columns even though no grid is
     * involved. Inert when not floating, since the box is 150px in a wider column.
     */
    margin-inline-end: var(--grid-gutter-content);

    /*
     * base.css caps every figure at the 66ch measure. Harmless here — 150px is far
     * inside it — but `max-inline-size` would still win over `inline-size` if the
     * measure ever resolved smaller than the thumbnail, which it does at the very
     * narrow end: 66ch of a scaled-down font could fall under 150px. Pinning both
     * keeps the thumbnail a thumbnail at every size.
     */
    max-inline-size: 9.375rem;
  }

  /*
   * ── The outline ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * DESIGN.md needed no new vocabulary for this. Elevation says depth is "tonal
   * layering plus a hairline border", and Shapes gives content images `radius-1`,
   * so the flag's own description — "help them not disappear onto the page at the
   * edges" — is served by the two tokens that already mean exactly that.
   *
   * ON THE IMAGE, NOT THE FIGURE. A `figure` also contains the caption, so a
   * border there would box the text in with the picture. The flag is about the
   * image's edges dissolving, so the border belongs on the image.
   */
  figure[data-outline] :global(img) {
    border: var(--border-width-hairline) solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-1);
  }
</style>
